fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1071", description: some(value: "cpp-gen emits a hyphenated anthill identifier VERBATIM, in every position -- measured, `int64_t zero-val;` and `namespace test::hy-phen` are not C++, and rust-gen/smt-gen already solved this.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-10 via `emit_namespace_header` on a probe namespace (anthill-cpp-gen/tests, integration harness). Source:\n\n  namespace test.hy-phen\n    sort my-widget\n      entity my-widget(zero-val: Int64)\n    end\n    sort Calc\n      sort T = ?\n      operation zero-val(my-arg: T) -> T\n    end\n  end\n\nemits, with no refusal:\n\n  namespace test::hy-phen {\n  struct my-widget {\n      int64_t zero-val;\n  };\n  template<typename T>\n  struct Calc {\n      static T zero-val(T my-arg);\n  };\n  }  // namespace test::hy-phen\n\nand `header_filename_for_namespace(\"test.hy-phen\")` answers `test_hy-phen.hpp`. FIVE positions are wrong at once: namespace segment, struct name, field name, method name, parameter name -- plus the header FILENAME, which is the one a build system trips over rather than the compiler. This is the WORST failure class: not a type error but a PARSE error, i.e. the emitter produced text that is not the language.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO BACKENDS ALREADY DECIDED THIS. rust-gen converts (`anthill-core/src/codegen/rust.rs`, `to_snake_case(\"zero-val\") == \"zero_val\"`, pinned by a unit test at :1986) and smt-gen sanitizes (`sanitize_smt_id`, anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs:2032, which maps every non-alphanumeric to `_` and says so in its doc-comment: \"anthill names use a-z, A-Z, 0-9, `.`, `_`, `-`\"). cpp-gen is the only backend of the three with NO identifier conversion at all -- grep finds no case converter and no sanitizer in anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs. So the gap is cpp-gen's alone, and the precedent for what to do is in-tree twice.\n\nSCALAND'S SCALA BACKEND JUST DID THE SAME FIX (WI-1054, delivered): `-` normalizes to `_` FIRST, then the backend's per-kind convention runs, so `zero-val` and `zero_val` reach the host as one name. Read `Names.normalize` / `Names.scalaPackageSegment` in scaland and `docs/scala-forward-mapping.md` §5 for the shape of the decision, including the two traps it hit: (1) a package/namespace segment needs its OWN rule, because it is also a DIRECTORY (here, a header filename) and cannot take an escaping treatment; (2) converting one side of a package-string comparison and not the other silently breaks name resolution -- convert at every producer.\n\nDECIDE, and say why, in `docs/cpp-forward-mapping.md` (or wherever cpp-gen's mapping is written -- if there is no such doc, that is part of this ticket): C++ has no camelCase convention the way Scala does and the existing emission keeps anthill spellings as-written (`struct Widget`, `static T compare`), so plain `-` -> `_` is very likely the whole answer here, unlike scaland where it composed with a camelCase rule. Whatever is chosen, the DOC and the CODE must agree at the end of it.\n\nCHECK THE WHOLE SURFACE, not just the five positions above: enum/variant names, the `namespace_map` of an Implementation fact, `CarrierBinding.sort_name`, and any place a host string is keyed by an anthill name -- a conversion applied at emission but not at lookup turns a working carrier binding into a silent miss.\n\nNOTE THE MANY-TO-ONE COST, which WI-1054 had to handle: once `-` and `_` are one name, `foo-bar` and `foo_bar` become one C++ identifier and can collide. scaland refuses the collision loudly (`Bootstrap.refuseColliding`) rather than emitting two declarations of one name; cpp-gen needs the equivalent or it ships a header that silently lost a struct.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a test that a hyphenated name in each position (namespace segment, sort, entity, field, operation, parameter) emits a legal C++ identifier, asserting the CHOSEN spelling rather than merely 'not a hyphen'. The header-filename path asserted too. Prefer driving it through the existing `header_compile_test` harness if that compiles emitted headers with a real compiler -- an emitter's capability is emitting COMPILABLE source, and a substring match tests the test author. A collision test if the decision is normalization. Say at each test site which assertion fails when the fix is backed out. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1054 (scaland scala-gen, delivered) -- the same defect class, found in the other backend while fixing that one."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-10T16:31:37Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1071", author: "codex", content: "Implemented the C++ identifier boundary as hyphen to underscore, preserving case, across namespace segments, generated header filenames, declarations, references, template parameters, and expression-local binders; carrier method casing composes after normalization. Realization lookup keys and host strings remain exact Anthill/host spellings, with a driven CarrierTable control proving no normalized lookup leak. Distinct Anthill spellings that normalize to one C++ identifier now fail loudly in emitted scopes. Updated docs/cpp-forward-mapping.md and CLI filename/controller consumers. Red focused run failed the verbatim-hyphen and collision cases; green focused integration passed 3/3 and compiled the emitted header with a real C++17 compiler. Full rustland/scripts/test.sh exited 0 with no failures; log rustland/target/test-run-20260810-175101.log. cargo fmt --all -- --check and git diff --check pass. Manual diff review found no issues. The repository-required /code-review skill is unavailable in this session, so it could not be run; manual review was used and this limitation is recorded explicitly. Open unblocked queue measured at 140 items.", at: "2026-08-10T16:31:27Z")
